The shim library allows Variorum to undergo unit
tests in the absense of hardware.  It does so by
intercepting C library (and other) calls and
returning values consistent with what would be
observed if the hardware in question were present.

This approach can be quite powerful, and we have
divided up the feature set along several axes.
Each feature set has several possible levels of
of sophistication.

I.  Error handling
    A.  All calls succeed.
    B.  Variorum-level errors are caught and
        generate expected error codes.
    C.  Arbitrary errors can be injected via
        environment variables.

II. Fidelity
    A.  All reads return a single magic number.
        All write succeed and are ignored.
    B.  Reads return realistic values for that
        manufacturer, writes are recorded and
        can be accessed with subsequent reads.
    C.  Reads return realistic values for the
        particular model.  Writes reflect the
        application of reserved fields.

III.System
    A.  Mimics a generic model from a single
        single vendor.
    B.  Mimics specific models.
    C.  Mimics underlying topology, CPUID.
    D.  Mimics multi-component system.


